    "lem-qftx-analytic-coherent-matrix-kernels": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal O_X)$ be a ringed space, so $X$ is a topological space and $\\mathcal O_X$ is a sheaf of commutative rings. An $\\mathcal O_X$-module sheaf $\\mathcal F$ is **locally of finite type** if for every $x\\in X$ there are an open neighborhood $U$ of $x$, a finite integer $r\\ge0$, and sections $s_1,\\ldots,s_r\\in\\mathcal F(U)$ such that their germs generate $\\mathcal F_y$ as an $\\mathcal O_{X,y}$-module for every $y\\in U$.\n\nFor an open $V\\subseteq X$, a finite integer $p\\ge0$, and an $\\mathcal O_X$-module morphism $\\phi:\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal F|_V$, its **relation sheaf** is the kernel sheaf $\\ker\\phi$. The module sheaf $\\mathcal F$ is **coherent** if it is locally of finite type and, for every such $V$, $p$, and $\\phi$, the relation sheaf $\\ker\\phi$ is locally of finite type. Here $\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus0}$ is the zero module sheaf.\n\nIn particular, for a finite tuple $f_1,\\ldots,f_p\\in\\mathcal O_X(U)$, its holomorphic relation sheaf is the kernel of the morphism $\\mathcal O_U^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal O_U$ that sends $(a_1,\\ldots,a_p)$ to $\\sum_{j=1}^p a_jf_j$. The structure sheaf is coherent when it is coherent as a module over itself. If $M$ is a complex manifold, an **analytic coherent module on $M$** means a coherent module sheaf over its sheaf $\\mathcal O_M$ of holomorphic functions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-kernel-cokernel-image-sheaves",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\varphi:\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal G$ be a morphism of sheaves of abelian\ngroups on a topological space $X$.\n\nThe **kernel sheaf** is the subsheaf\n$$\n\\ker(\\varphi)\\subseteq \\mathcal F\n$$\ndefined objectwise by\n$$\n\\ker(\\varphi)(U):=\\ker\\!\\bigl(\\varphi_U:\\mathcal F(U)\\to\\mathcal G(U)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nThe **cokernel presheaf** is\n$$\nU\\longmapsto \\operatorname{coker}\\!\\bigl(\\varphi_U:\\mathcal F(U)\\to\\mathcal G(U)\\bigr),\n$$\nand the **cokernel sheaf** $\\operatorname{coker}(\\varphi)$ is its\nsheafification.\n\nThe **image presheaf** is\n$$\nU\\longmapsto \\operatorname{im}\\!\\bigl(\\varphi_U:\\mathcal F(U)\\to\\mathcal G(U)\\bigr),\n$$\nand the **image sheaf** $\\operatorname{im}(\\varphi)$ is its sheafification.\n\nFor sheaves of modules on a ringed space, the same formulas are taken in the\ncorresponding module categories on each open set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let\n$$\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow \\mathcal F_{i-1}\\xrightarrow{d_{i-1}}\\mathcal F_i\\xrightarrow{d_i}\\mathcal F_{i+1}\\longrightarrow\\cdots\n$$\nbe a sequence of sheaves of abelian groups on a topological space $X$. Then the\nsequence is exact if and only if, for every point $x\\in X$, the stalk sequence\n$$\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow (\\mathcal F_{i-1})_x\\xrightarrow{(d_{i-1})_x}(\\mathcal F_i)_x\\xrightarrow{(d_i)_x}(\\mathcal F_{i+1})_x\\longrightarrow\\cdots\n$$\nis exact.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Induct on the target rank $q$. If $q=0$, then $\\ker\\Phi=\\mathcal A^{\\oplus p}$, generated by its $p$ standard sections (and equal to zero if $p=0$). If $q=1$, the images of the $p$ standard sections are a finite tuple in $\\mathcal A$, and [F1] says its relation sheaf, which is $\\ker\\Phi$ by [F2], is locally of finite type.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Assume $q\\ge1$ and the result for target rank $q$. Let $\\Phi:\\mathcal A^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal A^{\\oplus(q+1)}$, and let $\\pi:\\mathcal A^{\\oplus(q+1)}\\to\\mathcal A^{\\oplus q}$ drop the first coordinate. By the induction hypothesis, around any fixed $x\\in X$ the sheaf $\\ker(\\pi\\Phi)$ has finitely many generating sections $s_1,\\ldots,s_r$ whose germs generate every stalk on one neighborhood $V$ of $x$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Since $\\Phi(s_i)$ lies in $\\ker\\pi=\\mathcal A\\oplus0^{\\oplus q}$, write $\\Phi(s_i)=(h_i,0,\\ldots,0)$ with $h_i\\in\\mathcal A(V)$. By [F1], after shrinking $V$ the relation sheaf of $(h_1,\\ldots,h_r)$ is generated on $V$ by finitely many sections $t^{(1)},\\ldots,t^{(m)}\\in\\mathcal A(V)^{\\oplus r}$. For each $\\ell$, define $\\sigma_\\ell=\\sum_{i=1}^r t_i^{(\\ell)}s_i$. Then $\\Phi(\\sigma_\\ell)=0$ because $\\sum_i t_i^{(\\ell)}h_i=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "For any $y\\in V$ and any germ $v\\in(\\ker\\Phi)_y$, step 2.1 gives $v=\\sum_i c_i(s_i)_y$ for some germs $c_i\\in\\mathcal A_y$. The first coordinate of $\\Phi_y(v)=0$ says $\\sum_i c_i(h_i)_y=0$, so $(c_i)_i$ lies in the relation stalk generated by the $t^{(\\ell)}_y$. Hence $v$ is an $\\mathcal A_y$-linear combination of $(\\sigma_\\ell)_y$. Thus these finitely many sections generate every kernel stalk on $V$; [F2] and [F3] identify the resulting subsheaf with $\\ker\\Phi$, proving local finite type.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
