            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-kernel-cokernel-image-sheaves",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\varphi:\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal G$ be a morphism of sheaves of abelian\ngroups on a topological space $X$.\n\nThe **kernel sheaf** is the subsheaf\n$$\n\\ker(\\varphi)\\subseteq \\mathcal F\n$$\ndefined objectwise by\n$$\n\\ker(\\varphi)(U):=\\ker\\!\\bigl(\\varphi_U:\\mathcal F(U)\\to\\mathcal G(U)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nThe **cokernel presheaf** is\n$$\nU\\longmapsto \\operatorname{coker}\\!\\bigl(\\varphi_U:\\mathcal F(U)\\to\\mathcal G(U)\\bigr),\n$$\nand the **cokernel sheaf** $\\operatorname{coker}(\\varphi)$ is its\nsheafification.\n\nThe **image presheaf** is\n$$\nU\\longmapsto \\operatorname{im}\\!\\bigl(\\varphi_U:\\mathcal F(U)\\to\\mathcal G(U)\\bigr),\n$$\nand the **image sheaf** $\\operatorname{im}(\\varphi)$ is its sheafification.\n\nFor sheaves of modules on a ringed space, the same formulas are taken in the\ncorresponding module categories on each open set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-exact-sequence-sheaves",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a topological space. A sequence of sheaves of abelian groups on $X$\nis **exact** when it is exact in the abelian category of\n[[thm-abelian-sheaves-form-abelian-category]], equivalently in the sense of\n[[def-exact-sequence-and-short-exact-sequence-in-an-abelian-category]].\n\nThus a sequence\n$$\n\\cdots\\to \\mathcal F_{i-1}\\to \\mathcal F_i\\to \\mathcal F_{i+1}\\to\\cdots\n$$\nis exact at $\\mathcal F_i$ when the image sheaf of the first morphism equals\nthe kernel sheaf of the second.\n\nThe same language applies to $\\mathcal O_X$-modules on a ringed space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let\n$$\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow \\mathcal F_{i-1}\\xrightarrow{d_{i-1}}\\mathcal F_i\\xrightarrow{d_i}\\mathcal F_{i+1}\\longrightarrow\\cdots\n$$\nbe a sequence of sheaves of abelian groups on a topological space $X$. Then the\nsequence is exact if and only if, for every point $x\\in X$, the stalk sequence\n$$\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow (\\mathcal F_{i-1})_x\\xrightarrow{(d_{i-1})_x}(\\mathcal F_i)_x\\xrightarrow{(d_i)_x}(\\mathcal F_{i+1})_x\\longrightarrow\\cdots\n$$\nis exact.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be coherent and fix $x\\in X$. Local finite type gives, after choosing a neighborhood $U$ of $x$, a finite-rank free module sheaf map $\\pi:\\mathcal O_U^{\\oplus k}\\to\\mathcal F|_U$ that is surjective on every stalk. By [F1], its kernel is locally of finite type; after shrinking to $V\\subseteq U$, choose finitely many sections generating that kernel on $V$ and let $\\rho:\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus m}\\to\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus k}$ be the corresponding map. The sequence $\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus m}\\xrightarrow{\\rho}\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus k}\\xrightarrow{\\pi}\\mathcal F|_V\\to0$ is exact by [F2], [F3], and [F4]. Thus every coherent module has a local finite presentation.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Let $f:\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal G$ be a morphism of coherent modules and set $\\mathcal K=\\ker f$. Choose locally a finite-rank free epimorphism $\\pi:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus k}\\to\\mathcal F$. The composite $f\\pi:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus k}\\to\\mathcal G$ has a locally finite-type kernel by [F1]; its image under $\\pi$ is exactly $\\mathcal K$ on each stalk, since $\\pi$ is stalkwise surjective. Hence $\\mathcal K$ is locally of finite type by [F4]. For every finite-rank map $\\eta:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal K$, its composite with the inclusion $\\mathcal K\\hookrightarrow\\mathcal F$ has the same kernel; [F1] for $\\mathcal F$ makes that kernel locally of finite type. Thus $\\mathcal K$ is coherent.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Let $\\mathcal C=\\operatorname{coker}f$. It is locally of finite type because it is a quotient of the locally finite-type module $\\mathcal G$. To check its relation condition, fix a finite-rank map $\\gamma:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal C$ and a point $x$. Since $\\mathcal G\\to\\mathcal C$ is a sheaf epimorphism, choose local lifts of the finitely many images of the standard basis near $x$; they define $\\widetilde\\gamma:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal G$ lifting $\\gamma$. Also choose a local finite-rank epimorphism $\\delta:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus m}\\to\\mathcal F$. Define $\\epsilon:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus(m+p)}\\to\\mathcal G$ by $\\epsilon(a,b)=f(\\delta(a))+\\widetilde\\gamma(b)$. By [F1], $\\ker\\epsilon$ is locally of finite type. Projection of $\\ker\\epsilon$ to the second factor has image exactly $\\ker\\gamma$ on every stalk: an element of $\\ker\\epsilon$ maps to zero in $\\mathcal C$, and any stalk element $b\\in\\ker\\gamma$ has $\\widetilde\\gamma(b)\\in\\operatorname{im}f$, so a stalk preimage under $\\delta$ completes it to an element of $\\ker\\epsilon$. The image of a locally finite-type module is locally finite type, so $\\ker\\gamma$ is locally of finite type. This holds for every $\\gamma$, hence $\\mathcal C$ is coherent.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Consider a short exact sequence $0\\to\\mathcal F'\\xrightarrow{i}\\mathcal E\\xrightarrow{p}\\mathcal F''\\to0$. If $\\mathcal E$ and $\\mathcal F''$ are coherent, then $\\mathcal F'=\\ker p$ is coherent by step 2.1. If $\\mathcal F'$ and $\\mathcal E$ are coherent, then $\\mathcal F''=\\operatorname{coker}i$ is coherent by step 3.1.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-2",
          "step": "4.2",
          "claim": "It remains to assume $\\mathcal F'$ and $\\mathcal F''$ coherent. Local finite generators of $\\mathcal F'$ together with local lifts to $\\mathcal E$ of finite generators of $\\mathcal F''$ generate $\\mathcal E$, so $\\mathcal E$ is locally of finite type. For any finite-rank map $g:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus k}\\to\\mathcal E$, the kernel of $pg$ is locally of finite type by [F1] for $\\mathcal F''$; choose a local finite-rank epimorphism $\\gamma:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus m}\\to\\ker(pg)$. Exactness gives a map $\\delta:\\mathcal O^{\\oplus m}\\to\\mathcal F'$ with $i\\delta=g\\gamma$. By [F1] for $\\mathcal F'$, $\\ker\\delta$ is locally of finite type. On each stalk, $\\gamma$ maps $\\ker\\delta$ onto $\\ker g$: inclusion follows from $i\\delta=g\\gamma$, and if $s\\in\\ker g$, then $pg(s)=0$, so $s=\\gamma(t)$ for some $t$ and $i\\delta(t)=g(s)=0$; injectivity of $i$ gives $\\delta(t)=0$. Therefore $\\ker g$ is locally of finite type. Since this holds for every $g$, $\\mathcal E$ is coherent.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra",
            "construct"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The proof allows zero-rank finite free presentations and uses finite generators in step 1.1."
