    },
    "thm-qftx-gaga-analytic-oka-coherence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-complex-manifolds-and-holomorphic-vector-bundles",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$. A **chart** on a topological space $X$ is a homeomorphism\n$\\varphi:U\\to\\varphi(U)$ from an open set $U\\subseteq X$ onto an open subset of\n$\\mathbb C^m$ with its usual topology ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]);\ntwo charts $\\varphi,\\psi$ are **holomorphically compatible** when the\ntransition maps $\\psi\\circ\\varphi^{-1}$ and $\\varphi\\circ\\psi^{-1}$ are\nholomorphic on the open sets where they are defined\n([[def-holomorphic-function-in-several-complex-variables]]). A **holomorphic\natlas** is a family of pairwise compatible charts whose domains cover $X$. A\n**complex manifold of dimension $m$** is a nonempty Hausdorff\n([[def-hausdorff-space]]) second-countable ([[def-second-countable-space]])\ntopological space carrying a holomorphic atlas; the same maximal-atlas argument\nas for [[def-riemann-surface-and-holomorphic-atlas]] applies verbatim with\n$\\mathbb C^m$ in place of $\\mathbb C$: the union of a holomorphic atlas with all\ncharts compatible with it is again a holomorphic atlas, so one passes to the\nunique maximal atlas and calls its members holomorphic charts. Each chart is a\nhomeomorphism onto an open subset of $\\mathbb C^m\\cong\\mathbb R^{2m}$, so $X$ is\na topological manifold without boundary of real dimension $2m$\n([[def-topological-manifold-without-boundary]]), and for connected $X$ the case\n$m=1$ recovers Riemann surfaces ([[def-riemann-surface-and-holomorphic-atlas]]).\n\nA **holomorphic map** $f:X\\to Y$ between complex manifolds is a continuous map\nwhose expression in every pair of holomorphic charts is holomorphic\n([[def-holomorphic-map-and-complex-jacobian]]); equivalently, for every open\n$V\\subseteq Y$ and every holomorphic function $h$ on $V$, the function\n$h\\circ f$ is holomorphic on $f^{-1}(V)$. The forward implication is the\nholomorphic chain rule; conversely apply this condition to the coordinate\nfunctions of a chart on $Y$.\n\n**Induced smooth structure and complex structure.** Holomorphic functions on\nopen subsets of $\\mathbb C^m$ are smooth\n([[cor-holomorphic-functions-in-several-variables-are-smooth]]), so every\nholomorphic atlas is a smooth atlas ([[def-smooth-atlas]]) and $X$ is a smooth\nmanifold of real dimension $2m$ ([[def-smooth-manifold]]) whose smooth charts\ninclude the holomorphic ones. In holomorphic coordinates\n$z_k=x_k+iy_k$ the **complex structure** is the endomorphism field $J$ of the\ntangent bundle determined by $J\\partial_{x_k}=\\partial_{y_k}$ and\n$J\\partial_{y_k}=-\\partial_{x_k}$; it satisfies $J^2=-\\mathrm{id}$ and is\nindependent of the holomorphic chart, because a holomorphic transition map has\ncomplex-linear differential ([[def-holomorphic-map-and-complex-jacobian]],\n[[thm-chain-rule-for-holomorphic-maps-in-several-variables]]) and complex-linear\nmaps intertwine the two coordinate definitions of multiplication by $i$. A\n**Hermitian metric** on $X$ is a Riemannian metric $g$\n([[def-riemannian-metric-and-riemannian-manifold]]) satisfying\n$g(Jv,Jw)=g(v,w)$ for all tangent vectors $v,w$; its Riemannian volume density\nis written $\\mu_g$ ([[def-riemannian-volume-density]]). The complex structure\nmakes each tangent space a complex vector space, so the tangent bundle is a\nsmooth complex vector bundle in the sense of\n[[def-complex-linear-and-compatible-bundle-connections]].\n\n**Holomorphic vector bundles and Hermitian bundle metrics.** A **holomorphic\nvector bundle of rank $r$** on $X$ is a smooth complex vector bundle\n$\\pi:E\\to X$ of rank $r$\n([[def-smooth-vector-bundle-rank-fibre-and-trivial-bundle]],\n[[def-complex-linear-and-compatible-bundle-connections]]) together with an open\ncover $(V_\\alpha)$ of $X$ and smooth local trivializations\n$\\theta_\\alpha:E|_{V_\\alpha}\\to V_\\alpha\\times\\mathbb C^r$ whose transition\nmatrices $g_{\\alpha\\beta}:V_\\alpha\\cap V_\\beta\\to\\mathrm{GL}_r(\\mathbb C)$,\ndefined by $\\theta_\\alpha\\theta_\\beta^{-1}(p,v)=(p,g_{\\alpha\\beta}(p)v)$, are\nholomorphic. A **holomorphic frame** over an open set $V$ is a smooth\ncomplex-linear trivialization whose transition matrices with the supplied\ntrivializations are holomorphic on every overlap. Changing holomorphic frame multiplies a local section by a\nholomorphic matrix, hence a smooth local section is **holomorphic** exactly when\nits representatives in holomorphic frames are holomorphic functions. A\n**Hermitian metric** $h$ on $E$ is a smooth bundle metric\n([[def-smooth-bundle-metric]]) whose fibre pairings are positive definite\nHermitian forms, linear in the first variable and conjugate-linear in the\nsecond; the condition is frame-independent because the gauge freedom is\nconjugation by a pointwise invertible matrix. Smooth sections are\n$\\Gamma(E)$ ([[def-smooth-section-local-section-and-support]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal O_X)$ be a ringed space, so $X$ is a topological space and $\\mathcal O_X$ is a sheaf of commutative rings. An $\\mathcal O_X$-module sheaf $\\mathcal F$ is **locally of finite type** if for every $x\\in X$ there are an open neighborhood $U$ of $x$, a finite integer $r\\ge0$, and sections $s_1,\\ldots,s_r\\in\\mathcal F(U)$ such that their germs generate $\\mathcal F_y$ as an $\\mathcal O_{X,y}$-module for every $y\\in U$.\n\nFor an open $V\\subseteq X$, a finite integer $p\\ge0$, and an $\\mathcal O_X$-module morphism $\\phi:\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal F|_V$, its **relation sheaf** is the kernel sheaf $\\ker\\phi$. The module sheaf $\\mathcal F$ is **coherent** if it is locally of finite type and, for every such $V$, $p$, and $\\phi$, the relation sheaf $\\ker\\phi$ is locally of finite type. Here $\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus0}$ is the zero module sheaf.\n\nIn particular, for a finite tuple $f_1,\\ldots,f_p\\in\\mathcal O_X(U)$, its holomorphic relation sheaf is the kernel of the morphism $\\mathcal O_U^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal O_U$ that sends $(a_1,\\ldots,a_p)$ to $\\sum_{j=1}^p a_jf_j$. The structure sheaf is coherent when it is coherent as a module over itself. If $M$ is a complex manifold, an **analytic coherent module on $M$** means a coherent module sheaf over its sheaf $\\mathcal O_M$ of holomorphic functions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "def-stalk-of-presheaf",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be a presheaf on a topological space $X$, and let $x\\in X$.\n\nThe **neighbourhood category of $x$** is the full subcategory\n$$\\mathcal N_x\\subseteq \\operatorname{Open}(X)$$\nwhose objects are the open neighbourhoods of $x$. Its opposite category is\nfiltered in the sense of\n[[def-filtered-category-and-filtered-colimit]]: it is nonempty because $X$\nitself is a neighbourhood of $x$, and for neighbourhoods $U,V$ the\nintersection $U\\cap V$ is again a neighbourhood of $x$ with arrows\n$$U\\longrightarrow U\\cap V,\\qquad V\\longrightarrow U\\cap V$$\nin the opposite category.\n\nBecause $\\mathcal F$ is contravariant, its restriction to the neighbourhood\ncategory determines a covariant diagram on that opposite category. The\n**stalk** of $\\mathcal F$ at $x$ is the filtered colimit\n$$\\mathcal F_x:=\\varinjlim_{\\mathcal N_x^{\\operatorname{op}}}\\mathcal F(U).$$\n\nConcretely, $\\mathcal F_x$ may be described as equivalence classes of pairs\n$(U,s)$ with $U$ an open neighbourhood of $x$ and\n$s\\in\\mathcal F(U)$, where $(U,s)\\sim(V,t)$ when $s$ and $t$ agree on some\nsmaller open neighbourhood of $x$. The next lemma verifies that this is an\nequivalence relation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qftx-analytic-coherent-matrix-kernels",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A)$ be a ringed space whose structure sheaf $\\mathcal A$ is coherent in the sense that every finite tuple of its local sections has a locally finite-type relation sheaf. For all finite integers $p,q\\ge0$ and every $\\mathcal A$-module morphism $\\Phi:\\mathcal A^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal A^{\\oplus q}$, the kernel sheaf $\\ker\\Phi$ is locally of finite type.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "2.3",
            "2.4",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-qftx-gaga-analytic-bounded-degree-relations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n,q,d\\ge1$ be integers, let $U'\\subseteq\\mathbb C^{n-1}$ and $D\\subseteq\\mathbb C$ be polydiscs, let $w$ denote the last coordinate, and let $P_1,\\ldots,P_q\\in\\mathcal O(U')[w]$ be monic Weierstrass polynomials with $1\\le\\deg_w P_j\\le d$. For every $x=(x',w_0)\\in U'\\times D$, each germ in the relation sheaf of $(P_1,\\ldots,P_q)$ at $x$ is an $\\mathcal O_{U'\\times D,x}$-linear combination of relation germs whose components are polynomials in $w-w_0$ of degree at most $d$ with coefficients in $\\mathcal O_{U',x'}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.4",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-qftx-coherent-analytic-module-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal O_X)$ be a ringed space and use the coherence definition on the A page. The kernel and cokernel of any morphism between coherent $\\mathcal O_X$-module sheaves are coherent. In every short exact sequence $0\\to\\mathcal F'\\to\\mathcal E\\to\\mathcal F''\\to0$ of $\\mathcal O_X$-module sheaves, if any two of $\\mathcal F'$, $\\mathcal E$, and $\\mathcal F''$ are coherent, then the third is coherent.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-holomorphic-germ-ring-is-noetherian",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every integer $m\\ge1$, the holomorphic germ ring $\\mathcal O_{m,0}$ is a\nNoetherian commutative ring.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-finite-generation-and-finite-presentation-over-a-noetherian-ring",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a Noetherian commutative ring and let $M$ be an $R$-module. The\nfollowing are equivalent.\n\n1. $M$ is a Noetherian module ([[def-noetherian-module]]).\n2. $M$ is finitely generated\n   ([[def-generated-cyclic-finitely-generated-and-free-modules]]).\n3. $M$ is finitely presented ([[def-finitely-presented-module-and-algebra]]).\n\nThe proof below shows exactly where the Noetherian hypothesis is used to make\nconditions 2 and 3 agree.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-generic-linear-coordinate-makes-a-holomorphic-germ-regular",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$ and let $f\\in\\mathcal O_{m,0}$ be nonzero. Then there is an\ninvertible complex-linear map $T:\\mathbb C^m\\to\\mathbb C^m$ and an integer\n$d\\ge0$ such that the pulled-back germ $f\\circ T$ is regular in the last\nvariable of order $d$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-regular-holomorphic-germ",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $m\\ge1$, write $z=(z',z_m)$ with\n$z'=(z_1,\\dots,z_{m-1})\\in\\mathbb C^{m-1}$, and let $f\\in\\mathcal O_{m,0}$.\nFor $d\\in\\mathbb N$, the germ $f$ is **regular in $z_m$ of order $d$** if some\nrepresentative satisfies\n\n$$f(0,\\zeta)=\\zeta^d h(\\zeta)$$\n\non a neighbourhood of $0\\in\\mathbb C$, where $h$ is holomorphic and\n$h(0)\\ne0$. Equivalently, the one-variable slice $\\zeta\\mapsto f(0,\\zeta)$ has a\nzero of exact order $d$ at the origin.\n\nThe case $d=0$ is exactly the unit case $f(0)\\ne0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-weierstrass-preparation-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f\\in\\mathcal O_{m,0}$ be regular in $z_m$ of order $d$. Then there are a\nunit $u\\in\\mathcal O_{m,0}$ and a Weierstrass polynomial $W$ of degree $d$ such\nthat\n\n$$f=uW.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-weierstrass-polynomial",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $m\\ge1$, write $z=(z',z_m)$ with $z'\\in\\mathbb C^{m-1}$, and let\n$d\\in\\mathbb N$. A **Weierstrass polynomial of degree $d$** is a germ in\n$\\mathcal O_{m,0}$ represented by\n\n$$W(z',z_m)=z_m^d+a_{d-1}(z')z_m^{d-1}+\\cdots+a_0(z'),$$\n\nwhere each $a_j$ is an element of $\\mathcal O_{m-1,0}$, so for $m=1$ the\ncoefficients are complex constants, and $a_j(0)=0$.\n\nFor $d=0$ the lower-coefficient list and sum are empty, so the unique\ndegree-$0$ Weierstrass polynomial is $W=1$.\n\nIn particular $W(0,z_m)=z_m^d$, so every Weierstrass polynomial of degree $d$\nis regular in $z_m$ of order $d$ in the sense of\n[[def-regular-holomorphic-germ]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-weierstrass-division-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $W$ be a Weierstrass polynomial of degree $d$ in the variable $z_m$. Then\nfor every $f\\in\\mathcal O_{m,0}$ there exist unique germs\n$q\\in\\mathcal O_{m,0}$ and $r_0,\\dots,r_{d-1}\\in\\mathcal O_{m-1,0}$, meaning\ncomplex constants when $m=1$, such that\n\n$$f=qW+r_0+r_1z_m+\\cdots+r_{d-1}z_m^{d-1}.$$\n\nEquivalently, every germ has a unique quotient and a unique remainder of\n$z_m$-degree $<d$ upon division by $W$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-power-series-expansion-in-several-complex-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix $m\\ge1$, $a\\in\\mathbb C^m$ and a polyradius $\\rho$, let\n$f:\\Delta_\\rho(a)\\to\\mathbb C$ be continuous and separately holomorphic, let $r$\nbe a polyradius with $r_k<\\rho_k$ for every $k<m$, and let\n$C_k(t)=a_k+r_k\\exp(it)$ on $[0,2\\pi]$. For each multi-index $\\alpha$ set\n\n$$c_\\alpha:=\\frac1{(2\\pi i)^m}\\int_{C_0}\\!\\cdots\\!\\int_{C_{m-1}} f(\\zeta)\\prod_{k<m}(\\zeta_k-a_k)^{-\\alpha_k-1}\\,d\\zeta_{m-1}\\cdots d\\zeta_0 ,$$\n\nan iterated integral as in the polydisc Cauchy formula. Then, with\n$M=\\sup_{\\Gamma_r(a)}|f|$,\n\n$$|c_\\alpha|\\le M\\prod_{k<m}r_k^{-\\alpha_k},$$\n\nand for every real $\\theta$ with $0<\\theta<1$ the series\n$\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha$ converges absolutely and uniformly on\n$\\overline\\Delta_{\\theta r}(a)$ with\n\n$$f(z)=\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha\\qquad(z\\in\\overline\\Delta_{\\theta r}(a)).$$\n\nSince every $z\\in\\Delta_r(a)$ lies in $\\overline\\Delta_{\\theta r}(a)$ for some\n$\\theta<1$, the expansion holds throughout $\\Delta_r(a)$.\n\n**The coefficients are asserted here only as those iterated integrals.** That\n$c_\\alpha$ equals $\\partial^\\alpha f(a)/\\alpha!$ needs termwise differentiation\nand is not claimed by this statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "cor-uniqueness-of-multivariable-power-series-coefficients",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$, $a\\in\\mathbb C^m$ and let $r$ be a polyradius. Suppose\n$c,c':\\mathbb N^m\\to\\mathbb C$ both satisfy a bound\n$|c_\\alpha|\\le M\\prod_{k<m}r_k^{-\\alpha_k}$ and\n$|c'_\\alpha|\\le M'\\prod_{k<m}r_k^{-\\alpha_k}$, and suppose\n\n$$\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha=\\sum_\\alpha c'_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha \\qquad\\text{for every }z\\in\\Delta_r(a).$$\n\nThen $c_\\alpha=c'_\\alpha$ for every multi-index $\\alpha$. In particular a\nfunction has at most one such power-series representation about a given centre,\nand its coefficients are $\\partial^\\alpha_zf(a)/\\alpha!$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let\n$$\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow \\mathcal F_{i-1}\\xrightarrow{d_{i-1}}\\mathcal F_i\\xrightarrow{d_i}\\mathcal F_{i+1}\\longrightarrow\\cdots\n$$\nbe a sequence of sheaves of abelian groups on a topological space $X$. Then the\nsequence is exact if and only if, for every point $x\\in X$, the stalk sequence\n$$\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow (\\mathcal F_{i-1})_x\\xrightarrow{(d_{i-1})_x}(\\mathcal F_i)_x\\xrightarrow{(d_i)_x}(\\mathcal F_{i+1})_x\\longrightarrow\\cdots\n$$\nis exact.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "2.4",
            "3.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Work under [A1]. In a holomorphic chart, prove the relation assertion by induction on dimension $n$, and for a fixed $n$ by induction on tuple length $q$. On $\\mathbb C^0=\\{0\\}$ each relation space is a subspace of the finite-dimensional vector space $\\mathbb C^q$, so a finite basis gives local generators. For any dimension, the empty tuple has zero relation sheaf.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "If some tuple entry has zero germ at the chosen point, [F13] gives a neighborhood on which that entry vanishes identically; its coordinate in a relation is then arbitrary, and the relation sheaf is the direct sum of one copy of $\\mathcal O$ with the shorter tuple's relation sheaf. The induction on $q$ gives finite local generators, and repeating this finite reduction removes every zero germ. If some remaining entry, say $f_1$, is nonzero at the point, shrink until $f_1$ is a unit; the sections $e_j-(f_j/f_1)e_1$, $2\\le j\\le q$, generate the relation sheaf because the relation equation uniquely determines its first coordinate.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F13",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "It remains to treat a tuple of nonzero germs all vanishing at the point. By [F11], the product $G=\\prod_j f_j$ is a nonzero germ. Apply [F8] to $G$; after the resulting linear coordinate change the restriction of $G$ to the last coordinate line has finite order. Since this restriction is the product of the corresponding one-variable restrictions of the $f_j$, each factor is nonzero and hence has a finite order; each $f_j$ is therefore regular in the last variable.",
          "inputs": [
            "F8",
            "F11",
            "step 1.2",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "step": "1.4",
          "claim": "Apply [F9] to each of the finitely many $f_j$ and intersect the resulting neighborhoods. On one common product polydisc each $f_j=u_jP_j$, where $u_j$ is a unit and $P_j$ is a monic Weierstrass polynomial. Multiplication of the relation coefficients by the units $u_j$ is an isomorphism from the relation sheaf of $(f_j)$ to that of $(P_j)$. Set $d=\\max_j\\deg_w P_j$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F9",
            "step 1.3",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "By [F4], whose quotient/remainder step uses the uniqueness in [F10], every relation germ of $(P_1,\\ldots,P_q)$ is generated by polynomial relation germs whose components have $w$-degree at most $d$. Such vectors are identified with coefficient vectors in $\\mathcal O_{\\Delta'}^{\\oplus q(d+1)}$, where $\\Delta'$ is the base polydisc. Multiplication by the $P_j$ defines a finite matrix map $\\Psi:\\mathcal O_{\\Delta'}^{\\oplus q(d+1)}\\to\\mathcal O_{\\Delta'}^{\\oplus(2d+1)}$ by taking coefficients of $\\sum_j Q_jP_j$. Its kernel is exactly the bounded-degree polynomial relation sheaf.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F10",
            "step 1.4",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "By dimension induction, $\\mathcal O_{\\Delta'}$ is coherent; [F3] therefore makes $\\mathcal K=\\ker\\Psi$ locally of finite type. At the base point, its stalk is a submodule of $A^{\\oplus q(d+1)}$, where $A=\\mathcal O_{n-1,0}$ after translation. If $n>1$, [F6] makes $A$ Noetherian; if $n=1$, $A=\\mathbb C$ is a field and hence Noetherian. The finite free module is finitely generated, so [F7] makes it Noetherian and its submodule $\\mathcal K_0$ finitely generated.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 2.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "step": "2.3",
          "claim": "The sheaf $\\mathcal K$ is coherent: it is locally of finite type by step 2.2, and for any finite free map into $\\mathcal K$, composition with its inclusion into the finite free target of $\\Psi$ has the same kernel, locally of finite type by [F3]. A finite-rank free module over the coherent structure sheaf is coherent, since its standard basis gives finite type and [F3] gives the kernel condition. Choose finitely many germs generating $\\mathcal K_0$ and represent them by sections on one smaller base neighborhood. Their cokernel in $\\mathcal K$ is coherent by [F5]; its stalk at the base point is zero by the chosen generators and the stalk definition [F13]. Since that cokernel is locally of finite type, a finite generating family has zero germs there and vanishes after a common shrinking. Thus the selected sections generate every kernel stalk on a smaller base neighborhood.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F12",
            "F13",
            "step 2.2",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-4",
          "step": "2.4",
          "claim": "Regard these finite coefficient sections as polynomial relation sections on the product polydisc. Together with the finitely many elementary polynomial relations $P_qe_j-P_je_q$, they generate every bounded-degree relation stalk over the smaller base neighborhood; [F4] then makes them generate every relation stalk of $(P_1,\\ldots,P_q)$ throughout a smaller product neighborhood. The unit isomorphisms from step 1.4 transfer these generators back to $(f_1,\\ldots,f_q)$. Equality of the generated subsheaf with the relation sheaf follows from their equal stalks by [F12]. Thus the relation sheaf is locally of finite type at the chosen point.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F12",
            "step 1.4",
            "step 2.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Since the point and tuple were arbitrary, every finite tuple of holomorphic functions on every chart has a locally finite-type relation sheaf. A holomorphic coordinate change induces an isomorphism of structure sheaves and transports the relation map and its kernel, so the chart-local property is independent of coordinates. Hence [F2] makes $\\mathcal O_M$ coherent on all of $M$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F12",
            "step 2.4",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "step": "3.2",
          "claim": "Apply [F5] to the coherent structure sheaf $\\mathcal O_M$. It gives closure of coherent $\\mathcal O_M$-modules under kernels, cokernels, and finite extensions, as stated.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 3.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Fix coherent $\\mathcal F$, $x\\in M$, and finite $\\ell\\ge0$. Local finite type gives a finite-rank epimorphism $\\mathcal O^{\\oplus p_0}\\to\\mathcal F$ near $x$. The finite free source is coherent by [F3] and the coherence of $\\mathcal O_M$ from step 3.1; hence its kernel is coherent by [F5]. Choose a finite-rank epimorphism onto that kernel, compose with its inclusion into $\\mathcal O^{\\oplus p_0}$, and repeat this operation until $\\ell+1$ free modules have been chosen. At each intermediate stage the kernel is coherent by [F5], so the next finite-rank epimorphism exists locally.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 3.1",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-2",
          "step": "4.2",
          "claim": "Only finitely many neighborhoods are used for the finite length $\\ell+1$ construction; their intersection is a chart neighborhood $W$ of $x$. Each new free module maps onto the preceding kernel, so the resulting sequence is exact at $\\mathcal F|_W$ and every free term before the leftmost one. No further kernel is chosen at the leftmost term, and no global choices of generators or global resolution are asserted.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F12",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
