    "ex-qftx-gaga-analytic-skyscraper-presentation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal O_X)$ be a ringed space, so $X$ is a topological space and $\\mathcal O_X$ is a sheaf of commutative rings. An $\\mathcal O_X$-module sheaf $\\mathcal F$ is **locally of finite type** if for every $x\\in X$ there are an open neighborhood $U$ of $x$, a finite integer $r\\ge0$, and sections $s_1,\\ldots,s_r\\in\\mathcal F(U)$ such that their germs generate $\\mathcal F_y$ as an $\\mathcal O_{X,y}$-module for every $y\\in U$.\n\nFor an open $V\\subseteq X$, a finite integer $p\\ge0$, and an $\\mathcal O_X$-module morphism $\\phi:\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal F|_V$, its **relation sheaf** is the kernel sheaf $\\ker\\phi$. The module sheaf $\\mathcal F$ is **coherent** if it is locally of finite type and, for every such $V$, $p$, and $\\phi$, the relation sheaf $\\ker\\phi$ is locally of finite type. Here $\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus0}$ is the zero module sheaf.\n\nIn particular, for a finite tuple $f_1,\\ldots,f_p\\in\\mathcal O_X(U)$, its holomorphic relation sheaf is the kernel of the morphism $\\mathcal O_U^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal O_U$ that sends $(a_1,\\ldots,a_p)$ to $\\sum_{j=1}^p a_jf_j$. The structure sheaf is coherent when it is coherent as a module over itself. If $M$ is a complex manifold, an **analytic coherent module on $M$** means a coherent module sheaf over its sheaf $\\mathcal O_M$ of holomorphic functions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-qftx-gaga-analytic-oka-coherence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional complex manifold and let $\\mathcal O_M$ be its sheaf of holomorphic functions. For every open $U\\subseteq M$ and every finite tuple $f_1,\\ldots,f_q\\in\\mathcal O_M(U)$, the relation sheaf\n$$\n\\ker\\!\\left(\\mathcal O_U^{\\oplus q}\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O_U,\\quad(a_1,\\ldots,a_q)\\longmapsto\\sum_{j=1}^q a_jf_j\\right)\n$$\nis locally of finite type: every point has a neighborhood on which one finite family of relation sections generates the relation stalk at every point. Consequently $\\mathcal O_M$ is coherent. Coherent $\\mathcal O_M$-module sheaves are closed under kernels, cokernels, and finite extensions. For every coherent $\\mathcal O_M$-module $\\mathcal F$, point $x\\in M$, and finite integer $\\ell\\ge0$, there are a chart neighborhood $W$ of $x$ and finite ranks $p_0,\\ldots,p_\\ell$ for which\n$$\n\\mathcal O_W^{\\oplus p_\\ell}\\longrightarrow\\cdots\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O_W^{\\oplus p_0}\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F|_W\\longrightarrow0\n$$\nis exact at $\\mathcal F|_W$ and at every free term except the leftmost one. No exactness at the leftmost free term and no global analytic free resolution are asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qftx-coherent-analytic-module-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal O_X)$ be a ringed space and use the coherence definition on the A page. The kernel and cokernel of any morphism between coherent $\\mathcal O_X$-module sheaves are coherent. In every short exact sequence $0\\to\\mathcal F'\\to\\mathcal E\\to\\mathcal F''\\to0$ of $\\mathcal O_X$-module sheaves, if any two of $\\mathcal F'$, $\\mathcal E$, and $\\mathcal F''$ are coherent, then the third is coherent.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-kernel-cokernel-image-sheaves",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\varphi:\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal G$ be a morphism of sheaves of abelian\ngroups on a topological space $X$.\n\nThe **kernel sheaf** is the subsheaf\n$$\n\\ker(\\varphi)\\subseteq \\mathcal F\n$$\ndefined objectwise by\n$$\n\\ker(\\varphi)(U):=\\ker\\!\\bigl(\\varphi_U:\\mathcal F(U)\\to\\mathcal G(U)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nThe **cokernel presheaf** is\n$$\nU\\longmapsto \\operatorname{coker}\\!\\bigl(\\varphi_U:\\mathcal F(U)\\to\\mathcal G(U)\\bigr),\n$$\nand the **cokernel sheaf** $\\operatorname{coker}(\\varphi)$ is its\nsheafification.\n\nThe **image presheaf** is\n$$\nU\\longmapsto \\operatorname{im}\\!\\bigl(\\varphi_U:\\mathcal F(U)\\to\\mathcal G(U)\\bigr),\n$$\nand the **image sheaf** $\\operatorname{im}(\\varphi)$ is its sheafification.\n\nFor sheaves of modules on a ringed space, the same formulas are taken in the\ncorresponding module categories on each open set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let\n$$\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow \\mathcal F_{i-1}\\xrightarrow{d_{i-1}}\\mathcal F_i\\xrightarrow{d_i}\\mathcal F_{i+1}\\longrightarrow\\cdots\n$$\nbe a sequence of sheaves of abelian groups on a topological space $X$. Then the\nsequence is exact if and only if, for every point $x\\in X$, the stalk sequence\n$$\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow (\\mathcal F_{i-1})_x\\xrightarrow{(d_{i-1})_x}(\\mathcal F_i)_x\\xrightarrow{(d_i)_x}(\\mathcal F_{i+1})_x\\longrightarrow\\cdots\n$$\nis exact.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-stalk-of-presheaf",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be a presheaf on a topological space $X$, and let $x\\in X$.\n\nThe **neighbourhood category of $x$** is the full subcategory\n$$\\mathcal N_x\\subseteq \\operatorname{Open}(X)$$\nwhose objects are the open neighbourhoods of $x$. Its opposite category is\nfiltered in the sense of\n[[def-filtered-category-and-filtered-colimit]]: it is nonempty because $X$\nitself is a neighbourhood of $x$, and for neighbourhoods $U,V$ the\nintersection $U\\cap V$ is again a neighbourhood of $x$ with arrows\n$$U\\longrightarrow U\\cap V,\\qquad V\\longrightarrow U\\cap V$$\nin the opposite category.\n\nBecause $\\mathcal F$ is contravariant, its restriction to the neighbourhood\ncategory determines a covariant diagram on that opposite category. The\n**stalk** of $\\mathcal F$ at $x$ is the filtered colimit\n$$\\mathcal F_x:=\\varinjlim_{\\mathcal N_x^{\\operatorname{op}}}\\mathcal F(U).$$\n\nConcretely, $\\mathcal F_x$ may be described as equivalence classes of pairs\n$(U,s)$ with $U$ an open neighbourhood of $x$ and\n$s\\in\\mathcal F(U)$, where $(U,s)\\sim(V,t)$ when $s$ and $t$ agree on some\nsmaller open neighbourhood of $x$. The next lemma verifies that this is an\nequivalence relation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "1.4",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-power-series-expansion-in-several-complex-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix $m\\ge1$, $a\\in\\mathbb C^m$ and a polyradius $\\rho$, let\n$f:\\Delta_\\rho(a)\\to\\mathbb C$ be continuous and separately holomorphic, let $r$\nbe a polyradius with $r_k<\\rho_k$ for every $k<m$, and let\n$C_k(t)=a_k+r_k\\exp(it)$ on $[0,2\\pi]$. For each multi-index $\\alpha$ set\n\n$$c_\\alpha:=\\frac1{(2\\pi i)^m}\\int_{C_0}\\!\\cdots\\!\\int_{C_{m-1}} f(\\zeta)\\prod_{k<m}(\\zeta_k-a_k)^{-\\alpha_k-1}\\,d\\zeta_{m-1}\\cdots d\\zeta_0 ,$$\n\nan iterated integral as in the polydisc Cauchy formula. Then, with\n$M=\\sup_{\\Gamma_r(a)}|f|$,\n\n$$|c_\\alpha|\\le M\\prod_{k<m}r_k^{-\\alpha_k},$$\n\nand for every real $\\theta$ with $0<\\theta<1$ the series\n$\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha$ converges absolutely and uniformly on\n$\\overline\\Delta_{\\theta r}(a)$ with\n\n$$f(z)=\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha\\qquad(z\\in\\overline\\Delta_{\\theta r}(a)).$$\n\nSince every $z\\in\\Delta_r(a)$ lies in $\\overline\\Delta_{\\theta r}(a)$ for some\n$\\theta<1$, the expansion holds throughout $\\Delta_r(a)$.\n\n**The coefficients are asserted here only as those iterated integrals.** That\n$c_\\alpha$ equals $\\partial^\\alpha f(a)/\\alpha!$ needs termwise differentiation\nand is not claimed by this statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.4",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-uniqueness-of-multivariable-power-series-coefficients",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$, $a\\in\\mathbb C^m$ and let $r$ be a polyradius. Suppose\n$c,c':\\mathbb N^m\\to\\mathbb C$ both satisfy a bound\n$|c_\\alpha|\\le M\\prod_{k<m}r_k^{-\\alpha_k}$ and\n$|c'_\\alpha|\\le M'\\prod_{k<m}r_k^{-\\alpha_k}$, and suppose\n\n$$\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha=\\sum_\\alpha c'_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha \\qquad\\text{for every }z\\in\\Delta_r(a).$$\n\nThen $c_\\alpha=c'_\\alpha$ for every multi-index $\\alpha$. In particular a\nfunction has at most one such power-series representation about a given centre,\nand its coefficients are $\\partial^\\alpha_zf(a)/\\alpha!$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.4",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "If $y\\in D$ is not the origin, choose an index $k$ with $z_k(y)\\ne0$. Then $z_k$ is a unit in the germ ring $\\mathcal O_{D,y}$, so the ideal generated by the coordinates is the whole stalk and $\\mathcal F_y=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "At the origin, evaluation $\\operatorname{ev}_0:\\mathcal O_{D,0}\\to\\mathbb C$ is surjective. Its kernel is generated by $z_1,\\ldots,z_N$: if $f(0)=0$, its convergent power series has no constant term, and grouping each nonconstant monomial according to the first coordinate with positive exponent writes $f=\\sum_i z_i g_i$ with convergent germs $g_i$; each of the finitely many grouped subseries remains convergent on a smaller polydisc. Conversely every such sum vanishes at $0$. Hence $\\mathcal F_0\\cong\\mathbb C$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "At a point $y\\ne0$, choose $k$ with $z_k(y)\\ne0$. If $a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_N)$ is a relation germ in the stalk from [F6], then $a_k=-\\sum_{i\\ne k}a_i z_i/z_k$, so\n$$\na=\\sum_{i\\ne k}(a_i/z_k)(z_ke_i-z_ie_k).\n$$\nEach vector $z_ke_i-z_ie_k$ is one of the stated pairwise sections or its negative. Thus those sections generate the relation stalk at every nonzero point, including when $N=1$, where the sum is empty.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "step": "1.4",
          "claim": "Let $a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_N)$ be a relation germ at the origin, so $\\sum_i a_i z_i=0$. We prove by induction on $N$ that it is generated by the stated pairwise sections. For $N=1$, $a_1z_1=0$ and the germ ring is a domain by [F5], so $a_1=0$. For $N>1$, restrict to $z_N=0$; the first $N-1$ coefficients form a relation among $z_1,\\ldots,z_{N-1}$. By induction subtract a combination of the pairwise sections with both indices below $N$, extending their coefficient germs independently of $z_N$. The resulting first $N-1$ coefficients vanish at $z_N=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "By [F5], each remaining coefficient has a convergent series divisible by $z_N$, so write $a_i=z_Nc_i$ for $i<N$. The relation becomes $z_N(\\sum_{i<N}c_i z_i+a_N)=0$. Since the germ ring is a domain, $a_N=-\\sum_{i<N}c_i z_i$, and the remaining vector is $\\sum_{i<N}c_i(z_Ne_i-z_i e_N)$, a combination of the stated pairwise sections.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "At every point, steps 1.3–2.1 show that the pairwise sections generate the kernel stalk. Therefore [F4] identifies their generated subsheaf with $\\ker\\mu$. The quotient sequence is exact by the definition of $\\mathcal F$ as a cokernel.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Under the assumed Axiom of Choice [A1], [F2] makes $\\mathcal O_D$ coherent. Repeatedly applying the finite-extension case of [F3] to the split sequences $0\\to\\mathcal O_D^{\\oplus(j-1)}\\to\\mathcal O_D^{\\oplus j}\\to\\mathcal O_D\\to0$ shows that $\\mathcal O_D^{\\oplus N}$ is coherent. The cokernel case of [F3] applied to $\\mu$ then makes $\\mathcal F$ coherent.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
